## Problem:
A customer reported that one of its employees, Taylor, can create products in the system
but is not able to modify them. The customer says that Taylor should be able to see and
modify the products but not change the company’s inventory.

Try to guess a single SQL statement that will result in the expected behavior. (The
database used is MySQL 8)

# The tables:

# 1. table: user_define
id: 1, 2, 3
name: Ariel, Taylor, Luka
# 2. table: role_define 
id: 1, 2, 3, 4
label: charge, analyze, produce_consume, manage

# 3. table: action_define
id: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
description: create bills, create products, view bills, view products, modify products, delete products

# 4. table: user_role_rela 
id: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
user_id: 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3
role_id: 1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4

# 5. table: role_action_rela 
id: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6
role_id: 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 4
action_id: 1, 3, 4, 2, 6, 5

# Taylor's Exact Initial Profile:
 
User Taylor (user_id 2)
Roles: role_id 2 (analyze) and role_id 3 (produce_consume)
Action Relationships (role_action_rela): IDs 3, 4, and 5
 
What these relationship IDs grant her:
Relationship 3 grants action_id 4 (view products)Relationship 4 grants action_id 2 (create products)Relationship 5 grants action_id 6 (delete products)

# Solution:

Based on the database tables transcribed, Taylor (user_id 2) has two roles: analyze (role_id 2) and produce_consume (role_id 3).

Currently, Taylor has access to "view products" (via role 2) and "create products" / "delete products" (via role 3). Taylor is missing the "modify products" action (action_id 5).

To grant Taylor the ability to modify products, you should link the action "modify products" (action_id 5) to one of Taylor's existing roles. Giving it to the produce_consume role (role_id 3) makes the most sense contextually.

# Here is the single SQL statement to achieve this in MySQL 8:

```sql
INSERT INTO role_action_rela (id, role_id, action_id) VALUES (7, 3, 5);
```
